{"task_id": "Mbpp/3", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef is_not_prime(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if a given number is non-prime based on mathematical definition.\n    Returns True if non-prime, False otherwise (prime or less than/equal to 1).\n    \"\"\"\n    if number <= 1:\n        return False\n    if number == 2:\n        return False\n    for i in range(2, int(math.sqrt(number)) + 1):\n        if number % i == 0:\n            return True\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/4", "solution": "```python\nimport heapq\n\n\ndef heap_queue_largest(numbers: list[int], n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies the n largest integers from a list and returns them in descending order.\n    It efficiently uses a min-heap to keep track of the largest elements encountered.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0 or not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    if n >= len(numbers):\n        # If n is greater than or equal to the list length, sort all numbers in descending order.\n        return sorted(numbers, reverse=True)\n\n    # Use a min-heap to maintain the n largest elements seen so far.\n    # The smallest element among the n largest will always be at the top (index 0).\n    min_heap = []\n    for number in numbers:\n        if len(min_heap) < n:\n            heapq.heappush(min_heap, number)\n        elif number > min_heap[0]:\n            # If current number is larger than the smallest in the heap, replace it.\n            heapq.heapreplace(min_heap, number)\n\n    # The heap now contains the n largest elements; sort them in descending order for the final output.\n    return sorted(min_heap, reverse=True)\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/6", "solution": "```python\ndef differ_At_One_Bit_Pos(num1: int, num2: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if two integer numbers differ at exactly one bit position.\n    This is equivalent to checking if their Hamming distance is 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    xor_result = num1 ^ num2 # XOR result has 1s where bits differ.\n\n    set_bits_count = 0\n    while xor_result > 0:\n        xor_result &= (xor_result - 1) # Clears the least significant set bit.\n        set_bits_count += 1\n\n    return set_bits_count == 1\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/4", "solution": "import heapq\n\ndef heap_queue_largest(numbers: list[int], n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the n largest integers from a list using a min-heap and returns them in descending order.\n    The heap maintains the n largest elements encountered so far.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        return []\n\n    # Use a min-heap to keep track of the n largest elements.\n    # The smallest element among the n largest will always be at the root.\n    min_heap = []\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        if len(min_heap) < n:\n            heapq.heappush(min_heap, num)\n        elif num > min_heap[0]:\n            heapq.heapreplace(min_heap, num)\n\n    # The heap now contains the n largest elements in no particular order.\n    # Extract them and sort in descending order.\n    # Popping elements from a min-heap gives them in ascending order.\n    largest_elements_ascending = [heapq.heappop(min_heap) for _ in range(len(min_heap))]\n\n    return largest_elements_ascending[::-1]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/3", "solution": "import math\n\ndef is_not_prime(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if a given integer is a non-prime number according to defined business rules.\n    Returns True if non-prime, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number <= 1:\n        return False\n    if number == 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Iterate to check for divisors from 2 up to the square root of the number.\n    # If a divisor is found, the number is composite and thus non-prime.\n    for i in range(2, int(math.sqrt(number)) + 1):\n        if number % i == 0:\n            return True\n\n    return False"}
