{"task_id": "Mbpp/92", "solution": "```python\ndef is_undulating(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if a positive integer is an undulating number, where digits strictly alternate.\n    This means adjacent digits are different, and digits at i and i+2 positions are the same.\n    \"\"\"\n    s_number = str(number)\n    n = len(s_number)\n\n    # Single-digit numbers are considered undulating as the conditions are vacuously true.\n    if n < 2:\n        return True\n\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        # Business Rule 1: Adjacent digits must be strictly different.\n        if s_number[i] == s_number[i+1]:\n            return False\n\n        # For numbers with 3 or more digits, enforce the a-b-a pattern.\n        # This checks if digits at positions i and i+2 are the same.\n        if i + 2 < n:\n            if s_number[i] != s_number[i+2]:\n                return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
