{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Union\n\ndef max_product(numbers: List[Union[int, float]]) -> Union[int, float]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum product of an increasing subsequence within a given array of numbers.\n    It uses dynamic programming to find the maximum product ending at each position.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i] stores the maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    # Initialize dp array with each number itself, as a subsequence of length 1.\n    dp = list(numbers)\n\n    for i in range(len(numbers)):\n        for j in range(i):\n            # If numbers[i] can extend an increasing subsequence ending at j,\n            # update dp[i] with the maximum product found so far.\n            if numbers[i] > numbers[j]:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] * numbers[i])\n\n    # The maximum product of any increasing subsequence is the overall maximum value in the dp array.\n    return max(dp)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "```python\ndef check_Consecutive(numbers: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if the numbers in the given list are consecutive.\n    Returns True if consecutive, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers or len(numbers) == 1:\n        return False\n\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    for i in range(1, len(sorted_numbers)):\n        if sorted_numbers[i] - sorted_numbers[i-1] != 1:\n            return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "```python\ndef check_Consecutive(input_list: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given list contains consecutive numbers.\n    Returns True if the sorted list contains consecutive numbers, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Return False if the input list contains fewer than two elements.\n    if len(input_list) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Create a new sorted list to check for consecutiveness.\n    sorted_list = sorted(input_list)\n\n    # Iterate from the second element to check if each is one greater than the preceding.\n    for i in range(1, len(sorted_list)):\n        if sorted_list[i] != sorted_list[i-1] + 1:\n            return False # Non-consecutive adjacent elements found.\n\n    # If the loop completes, all elements are consecutive.\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "```python\ndef _canonicalize_tuple(tup: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Converts a tuple to its canonical (sorted) form for consistent comparison.\n    Elements within the tuple are sorted to ensure order-agnostic equality.\n    \"\"\"\n    return tuple(sorted(tup))\n\ndef tuple_intersection(list1: list[tuple], list2: list[tuple]) -> set[tuple]:\n    \"\"\"Computes the intersection of two lists of tuples, considering tuples with same elements as identical.\n    The function returns a set of tuples, where each tuple is in a canonical (sorted) form.\n    \"\"\"\n    canonical_set1 = set()\n    for tup in list1:\n        canonical_set1.add(_canonicalize_tuple(tup))\n\n    canonical_set2 = set()\n    for tup in list2:\n        canonical_set2.add(_canonicalize_tuple(tup))\n\n    return canonical_set1 & canonical_set2\n```"}
