{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "```python\ndef find_kth(array1, array2, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the k-th smallest element from two sorted arrays.\n    Uses a binary search-like approach to avoid explicit merging.\n    \"\"\"\n    m, n = len(array1), len(array2)\n\n    # Ensure array1 is the shorter array to optimize the binary search range.\n    if m > n:\n        array1, array2 = array2, array1\n        m, n = n, m\n\n    # Binary search for the partition point `i` in array1.\n    # `i` is the count of elements taken from array1.\n    # `j` is the count of elements taken from array2, such that `i + j = k`.\n    # The valid range for `i` is determined by `0 <= i <= m` and `0 <= j <= n`.\n    low = max(0, k - n)\n    high = min(k, m)\n\n    while low <= high:\n        i = low + (high - low) // 2  # Number of elements from array1\n        j = k - i                    # Number of elements from array2\n\n        # Get the elements at the partition boundaries.\n        # Use float('-inf') and float('inf') to handle edge cases\n        # where a partition is at the start or end of an array.\n        L1 = array1[i - 1] if i > 0 else float('-inf')\n        R1 = array1[i] if i < m else float('inf')\n\n        L2 = array2[j - 1] if j > 0 else float('-inf')\n        R2 = array2[j] if j < n else float('inf')\n\n        # Check if the current partition is correct:\n        # L1 <= R2 and L2 <= R1 means that all elements to the left of\n        # the partition are smaller than or equal to all elements to the right.\n        if L1 <= R2 and L2 <= R1:\n            # The k-th smallest element is the maximum of the elements\n            # just before the partitions in both arrays.\n            return max(L1, L2)\n        elif L1 > R2:\n            # L1 is too large, meaning we took too many elements from array1.\n            # Adjust the search space to the left in array1.\n            high = i - 1\n        else:  # L2 > R1\n            # L2 is too large (or R1 is too small), meaning we took too few\n            # elements from array1. Adjust the search space to the right.\n            low = i + 1\n\n```"}
