{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "```python\ndef find_kth(array1: list[int], array2: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the kth smallest element from two sorted arrays.\n    Uses a merge-like approach to identify the element efficiently.\n    \"\"\"\n    ptr1, ptr2 = 0, 0\n    kth_element = None\n\n    while k > 0:\n        if ptr1 >= len(array1):\n            kth_element = array2[ptr2]\n            ptr2 += 1\n        elif ptr2 >= len(array2):\n            kth_element = array1[ptr1]\n            ptr1 += 1\n        elif array1[ptr1] <= array2[ptr2]:\n            kth_element = array1[ptr1]\n            ptr1 += 1\n        else:\n            kth_element = array2[ptr2]\n            ptr2 += 1\n        k -= 1\n\n    return kth_element\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/611", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Union\n\nNumber = Union[int, float]\n\ndef max_of_nth(matrix: List[List[Number]], n: int) -> Number:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum value within the specified column of a given matrix.\n\n    Args:\n        matrix: A list of lists representing the matrix. Each inner list is a row.\n                Elements are expected to be numeric (integers or floats).\n        n: An integer representing the zero-based index of the column to find\n           the maximum value from.\n\n    Returns:\n        A single numeric value (integer or float) which is the maximum in\n        the specified column.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input matrix is empty.\n        TypeError: If 'n' is not an integer.\n        IndexError: If 'n' is negative or if 'n' is out of bounds for any row\n                    in the matrix (i.e., a row's length is less than or equal to 'n').\n    \"\"\"\n    if not matrix:\n        raise ValueError(\"Matrix cannot be empty\")\n\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Column index 'n' must be an integer.\")\n\n    if n < 0:\n        raise IndexError(\"Column index 'n' cannot be negative.\")\n\n    max_value: Number = float('-inf')\n\n    for row_index, row in enumerate(matrix):\n        if len(row) <= n:\n            raise IndexError(f\"Column index {n} is out of bounds for row {row_index} with length {len(row)}\")\n\n        current_element = row[n]\n        if current_element > max_value:\n            max_value = current_element\n\n    return max_value\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/615", "solution": "```python\ndef average_tuple(input_tuple: tuple[tuple[float,...],...]) -> list[float]:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the average of elements within each inner tuple of the input_tuple.\n    Returns a list of these averages, considering empty inner tuples as 0 and an empty input tuple as an empty list.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not input_tuple:\n        return []\n\n    results_list = []\n    for inner_tuple in input_tuple:\n        if not inner_tuple:\n            average = 0.0\n        else:\n            average = sum(inner_tuple) / len(inner_tuple)\n        results_list.append(average)\n\n    return results_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/616", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Tuple, Union\n\n# Define Number type as specified in the function signature design.\nNumber = Union[int, float]\n\ndef tuple_modulo(tuple1: Tuple[Number, ...], tuple2: Tuple[Number, ...]) -> Tuple[Number, ...]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Performs element-wise modulo on two input tuples of the same length.\n    Returns a new tuple containing the results of these operations.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Per CRITICAL REQUIREMENTS, validation checks and exception raising are forbidden.\n    # We trust that tuple1 and tuple2 are of equal length and contain numerical elements.\n\n    # Compute the modulo for corresponding elements and collect results into a new tuple.\n    return tuple(t1 % t2 for t1, t2 in zip(tuple1, tuple2))\n```"}
