{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_subset(numbers: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the size of the largest subset of a list of numbers such that every pair in the subset is divisible.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        The size of the largest divisible subset.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0\n\n    # Sort the numbers to apply dynamic programming efficiently.\n    # If a % b == 0, then a must be greater than or equal to b.\n    # Sorting ensures we only check for divisibility in one direction.\n    numbers.sort()\n    n = len(numbers)\n\n    # dp[i] stores the length of the largest divisible subset ending with numbers[i].\n    # Initialize all dp values to 1, as each number itself forms a subset of size 1.\n    dp = [1] * n\n\n    # Iterate through each number in the sorted list.\n    for i in range(n):\n        # For each number[i], check all preceding numbers numbers[j] (where j < i).\n        for j in range(i):\n            # If numbers[i] is divisible by numbers[j], it means numbers[j] can be part of\n            # a divisible subset that ends with numbers[i].\n            if numbers[i] % numbers[j] == 0:\n                # Update dp[i] if including numbers[j] leads to a larger subset.\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n\n    # The maximum value in the dp array is the size of the largest divisible subset.\n    return max(dp)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/615", "solution": "```python\ndef average_tuple(tuple_of_tuples):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the column-wise average of numbers in a tuple of tuples.\n    Assumes all inner tuples have the same length and contain numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    num_rows = len(tuple_of_tuples)\n    # If there are no inner tuples, num_cols is 0, and the result is an empty list.\n    # Otherwise, determine num_cols from the first inner tuple.\n    num_cols = len(tuple_of_tuples[0]) if num_rows > 0 else 0\n\n    result_averages = []\n\n    for col_index in range(num_cols):\n        column_sum = 0\n        for inner_tuple in tuple_of_tuples:\n            column_sum += inner_tuple[col_index]\n\n        # Avoid division by zero if there are no rows, though input assumptions generally prevent this.\n        column_average = column_sum / num_rows if num_rows > 0 else 0.0\n        result_averages.append(column_average)\n\n    return result_averages\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/622", "solution": "```python\ndef get_median(list1: list[float | int], list2: list[float | int], n: int) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the median of two sorted lists of the same size.\n    It finds the two middle elements from the conceptually merged list and returns their average.\n    \"\"\"\n    i, j = 0, 0\n    m1, m2 = 0.0, 0.0\n\n    # The total elements are 2*n, which is always even.\n    # We need the elements at indices n-1 and n (0-indexed) of the combined sorted list.\n    for count in range(n + 1):\n        if i < n and j < n:\n            if list1[i] < list2[j]:\n                if count == n - 1:\n                    m1 = float(list1[i])\n                elif count == n:\n                    m2 = float(list1[i])\n                i += 1\n            else:\n                if count == n - 1:\n                    m1 = float(list2[j])\n                elif count == n:\n                    m2 = float(list2[j])\n                j += 1\n        elif i < n: # list2 is exhausted\n            if count == n - 1:\n                m1 = float(list1[i])\n            elif count == n:\n                m2 = float(list1[i])\n            i += 1\n        elif j < n: # list1 is exhausted\n            if count == n - 1:\n                m1 = float(list2[j])\n            elif count == n:\n                m2 = float(list2[j])\n            j += 1\n\n    return (m1 + m2) / 2.0\n```"}
