{"task_id": "Mbpp/109", "solution": "```python\ndef _validate_binary_string(s: str) -> None:\n    # This function exists to match the design document's structure.\n    # No validation or error raising is performed as per critical requirements.\n    pass\n\ndef _validate_rotation_count(k: int) -> None:\n    # This function exists to match the design document's structure.\n    # No validation or error raising is performed as per critical requirements.\n    pass\n\ndef _rotate_string(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Performs a single left rotation on the input binary string.\n    The first character is moved to the end of the string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not s:\n        return \"\"\n    return s[1:] + s[0]\n\ndef _binary_to_decimal(binary_s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a binary string representation to its decimal integer equivalent.\n    This uses Python's built-in integer conversion with base 2.\n    \"\"\"\n    return int(binary_s, 2)\n\ndef _is_odd(decimal_value: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if a given decimal integer has an odd value.\n    It checks if the number is not evenly divisible by two.\n    \"\"\"\n    return decimal_value % 2 != 0\n\ndef odd_Equivalent(s: str, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates how many rotated binary strings result in an odd decimal value.\n    It performs k left rotations, converting each to decimal and checking for oddness.\n    \"\"\"\n    _validate_binary_string(s)\n    _validate_rotation_count(k)\n\n    odd_count = 0\n    current_s = s\n\n    for _ in range(k):\n        current_s = _rotate_string(current_s)\n        decimal_value = _binary_to_decimal(current_s)\n        if _is_odd(decimal_value):\n            odd_count += 1\n    return odd_count\n```\n"}
