{"task_id": "Mbpp/124", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef angle_complex(real_value, imaginary_value):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the angle (argument) of a complex number in radians.\n\n    Args:\n        real_value: The real part of the complex number.\n        imaginary_value: The imaginary component. This can be a float (e.g., 1 for 0+1j)\n                         or a complex number with zero real part (e.g., 1j for 0+1j).\n\n    Returns:\n        The angle of the complex number in radians.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Create the complex number from the input parts.\n    # If imaginary_value is a complex number itself (like 1j),\n    # we take its imaginary part (which would be 1 for 1j).\n    # Otherwise, we use it directly as the imaginary component.\n    if isinstance(imaginary_value, complex):\n        z = complex(real_value, imaginary_value.imag)\n    else:\n        z = complex(real_value, imaginary_value)\n\n    # Calculate the angle using math.atan2, which correctly handles all quadrants.\n    # z.imag is the 'y' component and z.real is the 'x' component.\n    return math.atan2(z.imag, z.real)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/126", "solution": "import math\n\ndef _get_divisors(n: int) -> set[int]:\n    \"\"\"Finds all positive divisors for a given positive integer n.\n    It iterates up to the square root of n to find all divisor pairs.\n    \"\"\"\n    divs = set()\n    for i in range(1, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            divs.add(i)\n            divs.add(n // i)\n    return divs\n\ndef _find_common_elements(set1: set[int], set2: set[int]) -> set[int]:\n    \"\"\"Finds the common elements between two sets of integers.\n    This function leverages Python's built-in set intersection operation.\n    \"\"\"\n    return set1.intersection(set2)\n\n# Renaming the built-in sum to avoid conflict with the function named 'sum' as per critical requirements.\nbuilt_in_sum = sum\n\ndef sum(num1: int, num2: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of all common divisors for two given positive integers.\n    This function orchestrates divisor finding and common element identification.\n    \"\"\"\n    divisors1 = _get_divisors(num1)\n    divisors2 = _get_divisors(num2)\n    common_divisors = _find_common_elements(divisors1, divisors2)\n    return built_in_sum(common_divisors) # Using built_in_sum to avoid recursion with the function named 'sum'"}
