{"task_id": "Mbpp/232", "solution": "```python\ndef larg_nnum(input_list: list, n: int) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Identifies and returns the n largest items from a given list.\n    Handles various input validations as per design specifications.\"\"\"\n\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return []\n\n    if not input_list:\n        return []\n\n    # Adjust n if it's greater than the list length, effectively capping it.\n    n_to_select = min(n, len(input_list))\n\n    # Sort the list in descending order to easily pick the largest items.\n    sorted_list = sorted(input_list, reverse=True)\n\n    # Select the top 'n_to_select' items.\n    return sorted_list[:n_to_select]\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/232", "solution": "def larg_nnum(input_list: list, n: int) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the n largest distinct numbers from a list.\n    Handles n=0 and cases where n exceeds the number of distinct elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return []\n\n    # Get distinct numbers using a set, then convert to a list.\n    distinct_numbers = list(set(input_list))\n\n    # Sort the distinct numbers in descending order.\n    distinct_numbers.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    # Return the first 'n' elements from the sorted list.\n    # Python's slicing handles cases where n is larger than the list length gracefully.\n    return distinct_numbers[:n]"}
