{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "```python\ndef get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of possible integer sequences of length n.\n    Each element must be positive, less than or equal to m, and at least twice the previous element.\n    \"\"\"\n    dp = [[0] * (m + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n\n    # Base case: For length 1, any positive integer i up to m is a valid sequence.\n    for i in range(1, m + 1):\n        dp[1][i] = 1\n\n    # Fill the DP table for lengths from 2 to n.\n    for current_length in range(2, n + 1):\n        for current_element in range(1, m + 1):\n            # Iterate through possible previous elements.\n            # A previous_element `prev` must satisfy 2 * prev <= current_element.\n            # Thus, prev <= current_element // 2.\n            for previous_element in range(1, (current_element // 2) + 1):\n                dp[current_length][current_element] += dp[current_length - 1][previous_element]\n\n    # The total number of sequences is the sum of counts for all possible last elements of length n.\n    total_count = sum(dp[n][i] for i in range(1, m + 1))\n    return total_count\n```\n"}
