{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "```python\ndef max_Product(numbers: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the two integers in a list whose product is the highest.\n    This includes correctly handling negative numbers where two negatives yield a positive product.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Input Validation: Check if the input list contains at least two elements.\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list must contain at least two elements to form a product.\")\n\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # The two largest numbers in the sorted list\n    largest1 = sorted_numbers[-1]\n    largest2 = sorted_numbers[-2]\n\n    # The two smallest (most negative) numbers in the sorted list\n    smallest1 = sorted_numbers[0]\n    smallest2 = sorted_numbers[1]\n\n    # Calculate product of the two largest numbers\n    product_of_largest_two = largest1 * largest2\n\n    # Calculate product of the two smallest (most negative) numbers\n    product_of_smallest_two = smallest1 * smallest2\n\n    # Compare the two products to find the highest\n    if product_of_largest_two > product_of_smallest_two:\n        return (largest2, largest1)  # Return in sorted order as per example\n    else:\n        # If products are equal, either pair is fine. Return the smaller pair for consistency.\n        return (smallest1, smallest2)\n\n```"}
