{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "```python\ndef max_Product(numbers: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the two integers in an array that yield the highest product.\n    It assumes the input list contains at least two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list must contain at least two elements.\")\n\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # The highest product can come from the two largest numbers or the two smallest (most negative) numbers.\n    # Case 1: Product of the two largest numbers\n    product_of_largest_two = sorted_numbers[-1] * sorted_numbers[-2]\n    pair_of_largest_two = (sorted_numbers[-2], sorted_numbers[-1])\n\n    # Case 2: Product of the two smallest (most negative) numbers\n    product_of_smallest_two = sorted_numbers[0] * sorted_numbers[1]\n    pair_of_smallest_two = (sorted_numbers[0], sorted_numbers[1])\n\n    if product_of_largest_two >= product_of_smallest_two:\n        return pair_of_largest_two\n    else:\n        return pair_of_smallest_two\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/428", "solution": "```python\ndef shell_sort(arr: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Sorts the input list `arr` in place using the Shell Sort algorithm.\n    It returns the sorted list `arr` after applying gapped insertion sort repeatedly.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    gap = n // 2\n    while gap > 0:\n        for i in range(gap, n):\n            temp = arr[i]\n            j = i\n            while j >= gap and arr[j - gap] > temp:\n                arr[j] = arr[j - gap]\n                j -= gap\n            arr[j] = temp\n        gap //= 2\n    return arr\n```"}
